<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-1-20">
  <title>Resources for 1/20</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given a statement, explain why proving a <em>logically equivalent statement</em> also proves the original statement. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of the <em>contrapositive</em> of a conditional statement.  
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given a conditional statement, construct its contrapositive.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use the logical equivalence <m>P \rightarrow Q \equiv (\neg Q) \rightarrow (\neg P)</m> to prove a conditional statement by its contrapositive.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-contrapositive">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Proof by Contrapositive</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>proving theorems by contrapositive</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />

    <problem>
      <title>Introduction to Proof by Contrapositive(Not on Quiz)</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Consider the theorem we proved:  If <m>x \equiv 7</m> (mod 11), then <m>x+8 \equiv 4</m> (mod 11).
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    If you assume <m>x+8 \not\equiv 4</m> (mod 11), what can you conclude about <m>x</m>?
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    State what you found in (a) as a conditional statement.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Assume the statement <q>If <m>P</m>, then <m>Q</m></q>.
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    If you assume <m>Q</m> is false (i.e. <m>\neg Q</m> is true), what can you conclude about <m>P</m>?
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    State what you found in (a) as as conditional statement.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the truth tables for <m>P \rightarrow Q</m>, and <m>(\neg Q) \rightarrow (\neg P)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
    <problem>
      <title>Proof by Contrapositive</title>
      <p>
        Use a logically equivalent form of the statement to prove the following theorems.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the sum of two real numbers is less than 50, then at least one of the numbers is less than 25.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> are integers and <m>a</m> does not divide <m>bc</m>, then <m>a</m> does not divide <m>b</m>.  Hint:  Separate out one of the assumptions to make the contrapositive easier to prove.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Not on quiz) For all integers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>, if <m>m+n</m> is even, then <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are both even or <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> are both odd.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Not on quiz) Let <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> be integers.  If <m>a|b</m> and <m>a</m> does not divide <m>c</m>, then <m>a</m> does not divide <m>b+c</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Section 3.2: pp. 102-105
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>